<figure class="flow" aria-labelledby="r11-flow-title"><figcaption id="r11-flow-title"><span>Figure 1</span> Two lines travel towards a receipt</figcaption>
 <div class="tree">
 <div class="node result"><strong>Receipt result · 2 rows</strong><small>one row per line of order 103</small></div><div class="arrow">↑</div>
 <div class="node"><strong>Sort by line_no</strong><small>2 rows in → 2 rows out</small></div><div class="arrow">↑</div>
 <div class="node"><strong>Join on product_id</strong><small>2 line rows meet 3 product rows → 2 matches</small></div>
 <div class="inputs"><div><div class="arrow">↑</div><div class="node"><strong>Filter order_id = 103</strong><small>5 lines tested → 2 retained</small></div><div class="arrow">↑</div><div class="node"><strong>Scan order_items</strong><small>5 order-line rows</small></div></div><div><div class="arrow">↑</div><div class="node"><strong>Scan products</strong><small>3 product rows</small></div></div></div>
 </div><p class="caption">A conceptual physical plan over the fixed shop rows. Read input from the bottom towards the result. The join algorithm is left open here; Figure 2 changes how the matching is done. A native optimiser can choose indexes, another join order or another shape.</p>
</figure>
